<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · text</p><h1 class="enc-title">Hirlas Owain</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">12th century</span></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Hirlas Owain ('Owain's Drinking Horn') is a Welsh poem of the late twelfth century attributed to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/owain-cyfeiliog">Owain Cyfeiliog</a>, prince of southern Powys. Set at a feast after a raid, it has the prince call on his <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cupbearer">cupbearer</a> to fill the long blue horn for each of his warriors in turn, praising their deeds and mourning the fallen. It is one of the best-known poems of the Welsh princes' era.</p></div>
